{
  "schemaVersion": "0.7",
  "name": "astral-3d",
  "description": "The Thermo Astral analyser as a solved volume: five-electrode mirrors at the published potentials, TILTED ABOUT y so the two mirrors converge along the drift. THE CONVERGENCE IS THE MECHANISM rather than a tolerance - it breaks translational invariance, which is what makes the drift decelerate and reverse, and a multi-reflection analyser whose mirrors are parallel cannot do that however it is tuned. WHICH AXIS MATTERS: tilting the mirrors (about y) gives each reflection a z-impulse of theta times its fixed x-impulse; tilting the boards (about x, as this template did until measured) instead closes the gap the ion flies down, which decelerates the drift about three times more weakly and needed a 1.58 mm spacer where the mirrors need 0.27. MEASURED: the drift reverses at convergence 0.267 mm (bracketed 0.26 to 0.28) at the design condition, against a published 200 um - a tested prediction, bracketed by runs that took no part in the fit. The oscillation count is N = injectionAngle * driftLength / (eta * convergence), where eta is the fraction of the ideal specular impulse this mirror delivers - 0.578 measured here, not 1, so the period does not quite cancel. The form is exact (checked by the 1/c scaling and by the sharper alpha/c invariance, 0.5%); the coefficient belongs to the mirror, and is the figure of merit the electrode depths actually move. GUESSED: the electrode depths d1..d4, because no paper states them; the scan that appeared to constrain them was measuring ions striking metal, and a tilted mirror's impulse is independent of its depth, so they need a figure of merit that moves with them. KNOWN WRONG: the drift faces are declared neumann, which says the structure repeats along z - true while the boards are parallel, false the moment they converge. That is now bounded rather than feared: it is worth about 11 per cent and is converged at the zPad declared here, and grounding those faces instead pins the ion in z entirely. ALSO NOT MODELLED, and more consequential than the boundary: the ion foil - electrodes above and below the path at 0 to -20 V, described by Hamish Stewart as critical to controlling z-axis focusing. Every z-direction result from this template attributes the whole drift behaviour to the mirror convergence, because convergence is the only thing here that acts along z. If the real analyser focuses its drift with a foil, this model reverses a free drift where that one confines a focused drift, and the 1.33x agreement with the published spacer is between two different mechanisms. See docs/astral-handoff.md.",
  "parameters": {
    "ionEnergy": {
      "value": 4000.0,
      "unit": "V",
      "minimum": 100.0,
      "maximum": 20000.0,
      "description": "Nominal beam energy. Stewart et al.: 4 keV entering the analyser."
    },
    "convergence": {
      "value": 0.3,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 0.0,
      "maximum": 40.0,
      "description": "how much closer the two mirrors are at the far end of the drift than the near end - the spacer that sets the mirror tilt. THE PUBLISHED FIGURE IS 200 um AND THIS SHIPS AT 300, which is not a disagreement with the paper but a placeholder for the ion foil. The paper says the drift is reversed by the mirror tilt AS WELL AS refraction on the foil; this model has only the tilt, so it needs more of it. Mirror tilt alone reverses the drift at 0.267 mm here, so 200 um does not come back at all. Fitting the foil should bring this down toward the published value, and how far is the measurement that would confirm the foil model."
    },
    "injectionAngle": {
      "value": 0.022343931724278912,
      "unit": "1",
      "minimum": 0.0005,
      "maximum": 0.2,
      "description": "tangent of the injection angle to the mirror axis. 1.28 degrees is the design condition: oscillations = 2*drift/(2*capToCap*angle), and 24 of them out-and-back over a 335 mm drift needs this. A published prism angle of about 2 degrees would give 4, which is not a 30 m path."
    },
    "driftLength": {
      "value": 350.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 50.0,
      "maximum": 800.0,
      "description": "Extent along the drift. Published 310-360 mm."
    },
    "capToCap": {
      "value": 625.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 50.0,
      "maximum": 3000.0,
      "description": "Derived from published figures: 30 m over 24 oscillations, out and back."
    },
    "boardGap": {
      "value": 40.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 5.0,
      "maximum": 120.0,
      "description": "Board-to-board separation at the drift mid-point."
    },
    "boardThickness": {
      "value": 4.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 0.5,
      "maximum": 20.0,
      "description": "How thick each board is in y."
    },
    "vacuumPad": {
      "value": 6.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 0.0,
      "maximum": 60.0,
      "description": "Vacuum between the outermost metal and the solve boundary."
    },
    "detectorInset": {
      "value": 10.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 1.0,
      "maximum": 100.0,
      "description": "How far inside the far end the collecting plane sits."
    },
    "d1": {
      "value": 20.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 2.0,
      "maximum": 350.0,
      "description": "Depth at which U1 is reached, from the mirror mouth. FREE: no paper states the electrode lengths, and they are what the published coefficients were optimised against."
    },
    "d2": {
      "value": 50.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 2.0,
      "maximum": 350.0,
      "description": "Depth at which U2 is reached, from the mirror mouth. FREE: no paper states the electrode lengths, and they are what the published coefficients were optimised against."
    },
    "d3": {
      "value": 90.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 2.0,
      "maximum": 350.0,
      "description": "Depth at which U3 is reached, from the mirror mouth. FREE: no paper states the electrode lengths, and they are what the published coefficients were optimised against."
    },
    "d4": {
      "value": 130.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 2.0,
      "maximum": 350.0,
      "description": "Depth at which U4 is reached, from the mirror mouth. FREE: no paper states the electrode lengths, and they are what the published coefficients were optimised against."
    },
    "halfGap": {
      "expression": "boardGap / 2",
      "unit": "mm",
      "description": ""
    },
    "mouth": {
      "expression": "d4",
      "unit": "mm",
      "description": "Where each mirror opens toward the beam."
    },
    "boardOuter": {
      "expression": "halfGap + boardThickness",
      "unit": "mm",
      "description": ""
    },
    "domainY": {
      "expression": "halfGap + boardThickness + vacuumPad",
      "unit": "mm",
      "description": ""
    },
    "domainMaxX": {
      "expression": "capToCap + vacuumPad",
      "unit": "mm",
      "description": ""
    },
    "domainMinX": {
      "expression": "-vacuumPad",
      "unit": "mm",
      "description": ""
    },
    "midPlane": {
      "expression": "capToCap / 2",
      "unit": "mm",
      "description": ""
    },
    "detectorZ": {
      "expression": "driftLength - detectorInset",
      "unit": "mm",
      "description": "kept for reference: where a detector at the FAR end of the drift would sit. The shipped detector is behind the launch point instead, because an asymmetric-track analyser's ions drift out, reverse, and come back - catching them at the far end measures only the outbound half and hides the behaviour the device is named for."
    },
    "mirrorTilt": {
      "expression": "asinPi(convergence / 2 / driftLength)",
      "unit": "1",
      "description": "half the angle between the two mirror assemblies. Tilting the MIRRORS (about y) is what reverses the drift: each reflection turns the velocity by twice this, so the drift decelerates by 2*v_x*theta per reflection whatever the mirror depth. Tilting the BOARDS (about x) instead closes the gap the ion flies down, which also decelerates the drift but about three times more weakly - see docs/astral-handoff.md."
    },
    "zPad": {
      "value": 150.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 0.0,
      "maximum": 400.0,
      "description": "how far the solve domain and the electrode stripes run beyond the drift at each end. The reversal convergence moves 7.4% from 0 to 50 mm and 0.4% from 50 to 150, so this is converged; at zero the Neumann face sits 10 mm from where the ion turns round."
    },
    "foilVolts": {
      "value": 0.0,
      "unit": "V",
      "minimum": -20.0,
      "maximum": 0.0,
      "description": "the ion foil bias. Published as tunable between 0 and -20 V. SHIPS AT ZERO, so the foil is present in the geometry and does nothing until it is fitted - a default that makes a device worse is worse than no default, and nothing yet establishes the value."
    },
    "foilThickness": {
      "value": 2.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 0.5,
      "maximum": 6.0,
      "description": "how thick the foil electrodes are."
    },
    "foilInset": {
      "value": 10.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 0.0,
      "maximum": 60.0,
      "description": "how far the foil stops short of each mirror mouth, so it sits in the field-free region between the mirrors rather than reaching into a retarding field."
    },
    "foilGap": {
      "value": 8.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 2.0,
      "maximum": 18.0,
      "description": "half-gap from the ion path to each foil electrode. Penetration is governed by width measured in units of THIS, so it sets the scale the widths are read against."
    },
    "foilSlices": {
      "value": 16.0,
      "unit": "1",
      "minimum": 4.0,
      "maximum": 64.0,
      "description": "how many steps approximate the graded width. A convergence parameter, not a design one: the shape is a wedge and this is how finely it is cut. The box primitive has parallel faces, so a varying width is a staircase."
    },
    "foilSliceLength": {
      "expression": "driftLength / foilSlices",
      "unit": "mm",
      "description": "axial length of one step."
    },
    "foilMidHalfWidth": {
      "value": 16.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 1.0,
      "maximum": 60.0,
      "description": "half-width of the foil at mid-drift, where it is widest. Published figures show the foil as leaf shapes - broad in the middle of the drift and tapering to points at both ends - and that shape is why it does two jobs at once."
    },
    "foilEndHalfWidth": {
      "value": 3.0,
      "unit": "mm",
      "minimum": 0.5,
      "maximum": 60.0,
      "description": "half-width at each end of the drift. Wider means more negative on axis, so a leaf that is broad in the middle puts an on-axis potential WELL at mid-drift. A positive ion displaced either way along the drift is pulled back toward the centre - which focuses the drift, and also returns it. A monotonic wedge gives a constant force and no well, which is what this template carried before the published shape was looked at."
    }
  },
